            "heading": "What Is OSMF?",
            "toc_label": "Understanding OSMF",
            "anchor": "what-is-osmf"
          }
        },
        "text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Oral Submucous Fibrosis (OSMF) is a long-term condition in which tissues inside the mouth can become stiff and less flexible. It may cause burning while eating spicy food and gradually limit how widely the mouth opens. Areca nut products such as supari, gutkha and paan masala are important risk factors.</p>"
          }
        },
        "chapter_2": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Signs & Symptoms",
            "toc_label": "Signs & Symptoms",
            "anchor": "signs"
          }
        },
        "text_2": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Common signs include burning when eating spicy food, tightness inside the cheeks, reduced flexibility of the mouth tissues and difficulty opening the mouth fully. Pay attention to symptoms that persist, worsen or begin to affect eating or daily activities.</p>"
          }
        },
        "checklist_1": {
          "type": "checklist",
          "settings": {
            "items": "Burning while eating spicy food\nTightness inside the cheeks\nReduced flexibility of the mouth tissues\nDifficulty opening the mouth fully\nDifficulty chewing\nDifficulty moving the tongue",
            "columns": "2"
          }
        },
        "text_3": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<h3>Why Does My Mouth Burn When I Eat Spicy Food?</h3><p>Burning can happen when the lining of the mouth is irritated or sensitive. It can have several causes, including OSMF, so burning alone does not confirm the condition. If it keeps returning or occurs with cheek tightness or reduced mouth opening, arrange an assessment.</p>"
          }
        },
