        "chapter_5": {
          "type": "chapter",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can OSMF Turn into Oral Cancer?",
            "toc_label": "Oral Cancer Risk",
            "anchor": "cancer-risk"
          }
        },
        "text_5": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>OSMF is recognised as an oral potentially malignant disorder, meaning it is associated with an increased risk of oral cancer. It does not mean that everyone with OSMF will develop cancer, but regular professional follow-up matters. A persistent ulcer, unexplained lump, bleeding or another unusual oral change should be assessed promptly.</p>"
          }
        },
